[al:新概念英语（三）]
[ar:MP3 同步字幕版（美音）]
[ti:A Skeleton in the Cupboard]
[by:更多学习内容，请到VeryCD.com搜索“露珠”]
[00:00.34]Lesson 24 | 第24课
[00:02.44]A skeleton in the cupboard | “柜中骷髅”
[00:09.68]Who was Sebastian? | 塞巴斯蒂安是谁？
[00:11.87]We often read in novels how a seemingly respectable person | 我们经常在小说里读到，一位表面上道貌岸然的某个人或某个家庭
[00:17.06]or family has some terrible secret which has been concealed from strangers for years. | 却有着多年瞒着外人的可怕秘密。
[00:24.73]The English language possesses a vivid saying to describe this sort of situation. | 英语中有一个生动的说法来形容这种情况。
[00:30.61]The terrible secret is called 'a skeleton in the cupboard'. | 这个可怕的秘密被称作“柜中骷髅”。
[00:35.69]At some dramatic moment in the story, the terrible secret becomes known and a reputation is ruined. | 在故事中戏剧性的时刻，可怕的秘密被人知晓，名誉随之扫地。
[00:43.08]The reader's hair stands on end when he reads in the final pages of the novel that the heroine, | 读小说读到最后几页，读者会毛骨悚然：书中的女主人公，
[00:49.34]a dear old lady who had always been so kind to everybody, had, in her youth, poisoned every one of her five husbands. | 那位一向待人和蔼可亲的老妇人，年轻时竟然先后毒死了自己的5任丈夫。
[00:59.79]It is all very well for such things to occur in fiction. | 这种事发生在小说中倒无伤大雅。
[01:04.47]To varying degrees, we all have secrets which we do not want even our closest friends to learn, | 我们人人都在不同程度上有些连最亲密的朋友也不想让他们知道的秘密，
[01:11.76]but few of us have skeletons in the cupboard. | 但我们当中有“柜中骷髅”的人却为数不多。
[01:15.50]The only person I know who has a skeleton in the cupboard is George Carlton, and he is very proud of the fact. | 我所认识的唯一有“柜中骷髅”的人是乔治·卡尔顿，他还引以为荣呢。
[01:23.87]George studied medicine in his youth. | 乔治年轻时学过医。
[01:26.67]Instead of becoming a doctor, however, he became a successful writer of detective stories. | 然而，他后来没有当医生，却成了一位成功的侦探小说作家。
[01:33.77]I once spent an uncomfortable weekend which I shall never forget at his house. | 我曾在他家度过了一个很不舒服、永远难忘的周末。
[01:39.38]George showed me to the guestroom which, he said, was rarely used. | 乔治把我领进客房，说这间房间很少使用。
[01:44.02]He told me to unpack my things and then come down to dinner. | 他让我打开行李后下楼吃饭。
[01:48.33]After I had stacked my shirts and underclothes in two empty drawers, I decided to hang one of the two suits I had brought with me in the cupboard. | 我把衬衫和内衣放进两个空抽屉后，决定把随身带来的两套衣服中的一套挂进衣柜。
[01:58.38]I opened the cupboard door and then stood in front of it petrified. | 我打开柜门，顿时呆若木鸡地站在柜门前。
[02:03.32]A skeleton was dangling before my eyes. | 一具骷髅在我眼前晃动。
[02:07.06]The sudden movement of the door made it sway slightly and it gave me the impression that it was about to leap out at me. | 柜门的突然移动使它轻微地摇晃起来，给我的印象是它好像要朝我扑过来似的。
[02:15.32]Dropping my suit, I dashed downstairs to tell George. | 我扔下衣服，冲下楼去告诉乔治。
[02:20.40]This was worse than 'a terrible secret'; this was a real skeleton! | 这比“可怕的秘密”还要糟糕：这是一具真正的骷髅！
[02:25.85]But George was unsympathetic. | 但乔治却无动于衷。
[02:28.42]'Oh, that,' he said with a smile as if he were talking about an old friend. | “噢，那个啊，”他微笑着说道，仿佛在谈论一位老朋友。
[02:34.56]'That's Sebastian. You forget that I was a medical student once upon a time.' | “那是塞巴斯蒂安。你忘了我从前是学医的了。”
